      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "Switching the digits of an integer reverses its order. To convert a two-digit number $n$ to its reversed form, multiply it by $\\frac{10}{n-1}$ such that the reversed form is clear. We then add this to the original number (multiplying it by $\\frac{10}{n-1}$) to get $2n$. The following reasoning steps allow for the isolation of $n$ and allow us to find a solution. Let's start with $n$: $$\\begin{aligned} n &= 10a + b & \\mathrm{where \\ } a \\text{ is the tens digit and } b \\text{ is the units digit.} \\\\ & = \\frac{10ab + 10b + 20b + a}{10(a - 1)} \\\\ & = \\frac{10ab + a + 3b}{10(a - 1)}. \\end{aligned}$$ Isolating $a$ via algebra produces: $$\\begin{aligned} 10ab + a + 3b &= 2n(a - 1) \\\\ 10ab + a + 3b &= 20a - 20 \\\\ -ab - 14a &= -3b \\\\ a(14 - b) &= 3b \\end{aligned}$$ If numbers have the same property of being able to be expressed as the product of only a unique pair of factors in a particular form, they become \"magic numbers.\" It is found that the only pair of factors suitable for $a(14 - b)$ is $3$ and $4$, producing the following Magic System (MS):$$\\begin{array}{cc} & |a| \\\\ \\text{Smallest product} & \\text{Max product exceeding magic system} \\\\ \\hline 3 \\times 4 & 12 \\\\\\hline 6 \\times 2 & 12 \\\\\\hline 9 \\times 4 & 36 \\\\\\hline 12 \\times 1 & 12 \\\\\\hline 15 \\times 3 & 45 \\\\\\hline 18 \\times 2 & 36 \\\\\\hline 21 \\times 4 & 84 \\\\\\hline & \\end{array}$$ Now we want both the tens and ones digits of $n$ to be between $1$ and $9$ to be a two-digit number. To make $n$ an integer, the units digit of $n$ must be smaller than the tens digit. The only possible solution is the one-digit number $1$, which is the smallest two-digit positive integer that fits the criteria when its reversed value is $\\boxed{3}$.",
